  {
    "id": "2102043169756271059",
    "text": "Fuel margins stabilise as funds realise profits\n\nRefined fuel markets experienced some profit taking last week after margins swelled to a near-record high amid intensifying attacks on shipping and pipelines in the Middle East and continued drone attacks on refineries across Russia.\n\nHedge funds and other money managers have been much more bullish about the outlook for fuels such as diesel and gasoline than for crude as the conflicts have disrupted the production and distribution of refinery products.\n\nThe benchmark 3-2-1 crack spread for transforming three barrels of U.S. crude into two barrels of gasoline and one barrel of diesel has traded between $60 and $70 per barrel almost continuously since late July ...\n\nThis is a short extract from my newsletter emailed to clients earlier today. If you would like to read my newsletters in full, three times a week, you can subscribe via my website https://t.co/aW1Xb1u8xH",
